{
  "id": "eks-managed-control-plane-nlb",
  "name": "EKS architecture \u2014 managed NLB & multi-AZ control plane",
  "category": "Containers & Kubernetes",
  "difficulty": "Advanced",
  "description": "Separate reference based on the supplied NLB image: ONE EKS cluster, one Kubernetes API endpoint, AWS-managed NLB and representative API server replicas across three AZs; one shared etcd cluster with three illustrated members. Native private access and API-to-kubelet connectivity are represented by EKS-managed cross-account ENIs in two customer private subnets. Dashed/curved management lines illustrate logical paths, not fixed ENI-to-replica wiring or executed Kubernetes calls. API/etcd counts and topology are illustrative: AWS runs at least two API servers and at least three etcd instances; no dedicated API/etcd pair per AZ is implied. The endpoint is an address; the managed NLB is behind public access, not a shared multi-cluster endpoint. Native private access is not customer PrivateLink. The kubelet and kube-proxy boxes are worker-process descriptions, not independent AWS services. The extra workload Service snapshot provides the existing Ready-endpoint request test; API authentication, RBAC, ENI routing, scheduler/controllers, etcd consensus, target health and node/process failure propagation are not simulated. No secure tunnel protocol is assumed from the conceptual image.",
  "learningOutcome": "Separate multi-AZ replication within one cluster from multiple independent clusters. Understand public endpoint \u2192 managed NLB \u2192 kube-apiserver versus private API connectivity through EKS-managed ENIs, and API server \u2192 kubelet logs/exec. Test the workload Service independently by changing its Ready count or selector. Sources: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/best-practices/control-plane.html ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/cluster-endpoint.html ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-architecture.html",
